<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  BrokenApp: a solution that loads only PARTIALLY, and must stay that way.

  BrokenApp.sln names three projects and this tree contains two. BrokenApp.Contracts is absent by
  design, and this project both lists it as a ProjectReference below and derives from, implements and
  is attributed with types it would have declared. Loading the solution therefore produces the exact
  field shape no other fixture here can:

    - a workspace-LOAD FAILURE — the project file the solution names is not on disk — so the model is
      missing a whole project rather than being merely small; and
    - Roslyn ERROR SYMBOLS inside the project that did load, because its reference to the missing one
      resolves to nothing. Those reach extraction's external-mint path through the base type, the
      interface and the attribute class, which is where a survey over such a tree used to die with an
      invariant violation naming a symbol nobody wrote.

  WHY NOT SIMPLY LEAVE IT UNRESTORED, to get the LOAD failure. Measured, and it does not work: an
  SDK-style project with no obj/project.assets.json still completes MSBuildWorkspace's design-time
  build and loads with ZERO workspace diagnostics. Neither does a missing MSBuild <Import> — Roslyn's
  build host evaluates projects with missing imports ignored, so that produces no diagnostic either. A
  project file the solution names but the tree does not contain is the failure the loader actually
  reports.

  THE TREE IS ALSO UNRESTORED, AND THAT NOW COUNTS — as the other cause. RestoreFailures blames an
  SDK-style project with no assets file, because such a project writes one on every restore, so this
  project and BrokenApp.Core carry the restore cause while BrokenApp.Contracts carries the load one.
  That makes this the only bed where both blocks of a refusal are composed over a real tree, which
  PartialLoadWorkspaceE2ETests asserts the ordering of. Do not "fix" it by restoring: constraint 3
  below forbids the obj/, and this project could not be restored in isolation anyway.

  THREE SITING CONSTRAINTS. Do not "tidy" any of them away.

  1. It lives under Fixtures/PartialLoadSolutions/, NOT Fixtures/TestSolutions/. FixtureRestorer
     restores every .sln under TestSolutions/** unconditionally at pipeline startup, keyed on "any
     csproj missing obj/project.assets.json". A solution that can never restore cleanly, sited there,
     would make every test process believe the tree is unrestored and re-restore MyApp along with it,
     once per run, forever.

  2. It is NOT a member of SlideRule.slnx and is NOT ProjectReference'd from the tests csproj.
     Either would try to build it, and a ProjectReference would additionally bake its resolved path
     into assembly metadata. It is a pure content fixture, copied to the test output by the tests
     csproj's Fixtures/** glob.

  3. Source only — no bin/, no obj/, no committed DLL, and above all NO BrokenApp.Contracts/
     DIRECTORY. Creating one is the single edit that silently disarms every test in
     PartialLoadWorkspaceE2ETests, which is why that class asserts its absence at arrange time and
     fails loudly rather than passing vacuously.
-->
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
        <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
        <!-- No audit: an unreachable feed's NU1900 warnings would ride the same diagnostics stream as the
             load failure this fixture is about, and the gate's audit carve-out would then be doing the work
             the load failure is supposed to do. -->
        <NuGetAudit>false</NuGetAudit>
        <RestoreLockedMode Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</RestoreLockedMode>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\BrokenApp.Contracts\BrokenApp.Contracts.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\BrokenApp.Core\BrokenApp.Core.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>
</Project>
